# Mandatory access control with SELinux.
#
# The kernel side costs nothing: the stock Arch kernel is built with
# CONFIG_SECURITY_SELINUX=y and CONFIG_SECURITY_SELINUX_BOOTPARAM=y, but
# CONFIG_LSM lists only "landlock,lockdown,yama,integrity,bpf", so SELinux is
# compiled in and inactive until `lsm=` on the kernel command line names it.
# install/server/selinux-server.sh writes that drop-in. No kernel is rebuilt.
#
# The userland is a different story, and this is the honest cost of the route:
# the first block is additive, the second REPLACES packages the base already
# has. Every one of them is built from https://github.com/archlinuxhardened/
# selinux at a pinned commit and signed into [omarchy-server];
# omarchy-server-pkgs/pkgbuilds/selinux.manifest records why each is in the
# set and why the ones that are not are not.
#
# Mutually exclusive with the `apparmor` addon: both are major LSMs and the
# kernel runs one of them at a time. The setup leaf refuses to install over the
# other one.

# --- additive: nothing here replaces a stock package -------------------------
libsepol
libselinux
checkpolicy
secilc
setools
libsemanage
semodule-utils
policycoreutils
# selinux-python brings semanage and audit2allow, and setools (its dependency)
# brings python-networkx, which in Arch hard-depends on python-scipy,
# python-pandas, python-matplotlib and python-numpy. Measured on an installed
# machine: those two packages and their closure are 45 packages and 453 MiB,
# which is 82% of this addon's entire size cost. See
# reports/2026-08-29-mandatory-access-control.md; splitting them into a second
# addon is the open recommendation.
selinux-python
selinux-alpm-hook
selinux-refpolicy-arch

# --- replacements: each provides= and conflicts= its stock counterpart -------
# pacman will not resolve a conflict under --noconfirm on its own, so this
# addon carries selinux.pacman-args, which adds `--ask=4` to pre-answer the
# replacement question. omarchy-server-addon reads that file.
pambase-selinux
pam-selinux
coreutils-selinux
# util-linux-libs-selinux is the other half of util-linux's split build, and it
# replaces util-linux-libs, which the base has. Named explicitly rather than
# left to dependency resolution, because the ISO reads this list to decide what
# to bundle in its offline mirror.
util-linux-libs-selinux
util-linux-selinux
shadow-selinux
sudo-selinux
openssh-selinux
systemd-selinux
systemd-libs-selinux
systemd-sysvcompat-selinux
